00:05:20.139 --> 00:05:21.139
So.

00:05:21.139 --> 00:05:26.389
None of the items recovered from police searches
-- the rental car, Joyce’s apartment -- linked

00:05:26.389 --> 00:05:28.050
her to the crime.

00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:34.900
But when evidence starts to suggest that weird
things are coincidences, it’s tempting to…

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:37.310
just come up with new evidence.

00:05:37.310 --> 00:05:42.889
Suddenly a woman named Martha Jean Bruce claimed
that Joyce had confessed to her while they

00:05:42.889 --> 00:05:46.349
shared a jail cell after Joyce’s arrest.

00:05:46.349 --> 00:05:52.110
Bruce said there was no incentive for her
to testify to that, but remarkably a few months

00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:59.560
later the DA recommended that Bruce be released
over 3 years early on a 5-year attempted murder

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:00.560
sentence.

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:02.560
So yeah.

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:05.830
The DA’s investigator Adi Christian found
her story convincing.

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:10.509
Well, after I talked to her a little bit in
detail she began to tell me some things that

00:06:10.509 --> 00:06:14.830
indicated to me that she would’ve had to
have talked to someone involved in that crime

00:06:14.830 --> 00:06:16.680
because of the facts she had.

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:22.129
About the way the furs were supposedly baggaged
when they left the scene.

00:06:22.129 --> 00:06:25.819
And Martha Jean Bruce had that information,
she knew that they were in plastic bags.

00:06:25.819 --> 00:06:27.750
But she could’ve read that in the newspapers.

00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:33.789
Oh, and Martha Jean Bruce had already been
convicted of lying to police.

00:06:33.789 --> 00:06:36.789
But the prosecutors ignored that.

00:06:36.789 --> 00:06:43.509
The odds of it all being the universe’s
cruel joke against Joyce Ann Brown were just...

00:06:43.509 --> 00:06:45.259
too much for the jury.

00:06:45.259 --> 00:06:51.370
She was found guilty and sentenced -- and
there was no hope the calculus was ever gonna

00:06:51.370 --> 00:06:54.029
be in her favor.

00:06:54.029 --> 00:06:58.719
Everything added up for the prosecution, and
the pattern checked out.

00:06:58.719 --> 00:07:04.479
Their case went like this: Joyce Ann Brown
is the black former prostitute in Texas accused

00:07:04.479 --> 00:07:08.879
of involvement in the robbery and murder of
a holocaust survivor on her lunch break from

00:07:08.879 --> 00:07:14.789
work at the same type of store a few miles
away, with the surviving victim visually identifying

00:07:14.789 --> 00:07:21.189
her, sworn testimony of a jailhouse confession,
and the getaway car rented in her exact name.

00:07:21.189 --> 00:07:23.479
Here’s a question.

00:07:23.479 --> 00:07:29.189
What are the odds someone with YOUR name commits
a crime?

00:07:29.189 --> 00:07:33.810
Since there are only 10 other Kevin Liebers,
I’m pretty safe.

00:07:33.810 --> 00:07:35.939
But what if your name is…

00:07:35.939 --> 00:07:37.710
Michael Stevens?

00:07:37.710 --> 00:07:44.469
There are 2,975 total Michael Stevenses in
the United States.

00:07:44.469 --> 00:07:46.659
How about John Smith?

00:07:46.659 --> 00:07:53.360
He’s got 48,439 other potential criminal
John Smiths.

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:59.729
Conversely, Tanner Butt’s innocence is firmly
up to him.

00:07:59.729 --> 00:08:05.839
6 months after Joyce’s conviction, the other
woman linked to the robbery and murder was

00:08:05.839 --> 00:08:08.050
finally apprehended.

00:08:08.050 --> 00:08:09.889
Rene Taylor.

00:08:09.889 --> 00:08:14.120
Police found furs and the murder weapon in
her apartment.

00:08:14.120 --> 00:08:20.180
She told police that she had no idea who Joyce
Ann Brown was… but she refused to give up

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:26.379
the name of her actual accomplice because
she didn’t want to ruin that woman’s life.

00:08:26.379 --> 00:08:32.490
Rene Taylor was convicted of murder and sent
to the Mountain View maximum security prison,

00:08:32.490 --> 00:08:34.470
where she served time with…

00:08:34.470 --> 00:08:36.550
Joyce Ann Brown.

00:08:36.550 --> 00:08:40.410
Guards were ordered to keep the two women
apart.

00:08:40.410 --> 00:08:41.960
Joyce is in prison.

00:08:41.960 --> 00:08:44.030
Rene Taylor is in prison.

00:08:44.030 --> 00:08:51.340
But It took years to determine who Rene’s
real accomplice was -- a woman named Lorraine

00:08:51.340 --> 00:08:57.510
Germany who’d also robbed an Albuquerque,
New Mexico fur store with Taylor -- and an

00:08:57.510 --> 00:09:05.040
investigator told Joyce that “Lorraine looked
enough like you to be your twin sister.”

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:06.120
Name.

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:07.200
Furs.

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:08.280
DOPPELGANGER.

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:09.370
Ridiculous.

00:09:09.370 --> 00:09:15.680
In 1984, Joyce’s appeal was denied on her
birthday.

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:19.730
2 weeks later, her son shot himself.

00:09:19.730 --> 00:09:24.660
His last words to her were, “Mama, when
are they going to let you out?

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:27.090
We don’t have a family anymore.”

00:09:27.090 --> 00:09:33.720
Most crimes aren’t all THAT different from
each other, and the details usually add up.

00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:36.080
There are often familiar patterns.

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:43.080
The Dallas police fell victim to the Einstellung
Effect, a cognitive phenomenon detailed in

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:46.240
my video "Can Learning Make You Dumb?"

00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:49.610
They had tunnel vision based on their experience.

00:09:49.610 --> 00:09:56.050
They also had a lot of possible evidence to
choose from, so they only saw what they wanted

00:09:56.050 --> 00:10:00.490
to see -- and they wanted to see it in a certain
way.

00:10:00.490 --> 00:10:05.290
It’s the same mistake we saw with Brandon
Mayfield’s fingerprint case.

00:10:05.290 --> 00:10:13.410
And just like Sally Clark’s trial, the prosecution
and jury refused to admit that long odds and

00:10:13.410 --> 00:10:19.210
crazy coincidences really do happen -- they
just do.

00:10:19.210 --> 00:10:23.670
Or maybe they just didn’t understand that
they do.

00:10:23.670 --> 00:10:29.030
One way to combat the Einstellung Effect is
to bring in people to look at the problem

00:10:29.030 --> 00:10:32.750
who aren’t used to operating in familiar
patterns.

00:10:32.750 --> 00:10:38.060
Dallas Morning News journalists Steve McGonigal
and Steve Blow started questioning the district

00:10:38.060 --> 00:10:39.800
attorney’s narrative.

00:10:39.800 --> 00:10:45.350
Jim McCloskey of Centurion Ministries in Princeton,
New Jersey and his own investigator started

00:10:45.350 --> 00:10:49.000
putting the evidence together to get Joyce
exonerated.

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:57.050
And in 1989, just weeks after Morley Safer
of 60 Minutes aired a 14-minute piece detailing

00:10:57.050 --> 00:11:03.240
the doubt surrounding Joyce’s conviction,
she was finally granted a retrial.

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:12.830
For the first time in 9 years, 5 months, and
24 days, Joyce was free -- and months later,

00:11:12.830 --> 00:11:15.880
the DA dropped the case against her.

00:11:15.880 --> 00:11:20.990
Joyce Ann Brown spent the years following
her release working as an assistant to a county

00:11:20.990 --> 00:11:27.670
official before founding an organization to
assist the incarcerated and their families.

00:11:27.670 --> 00:11:30.020
She died of a heart attack in 2015.

00:11:30.020 --> 00:11:38.260
Joyce’s strength and character allowed her
to turn her injustice into a springboard to

00:11:38.260 --> 00:11:39.880
help other people.

00:11:39.880 --> 00:11:44.630
But… she never should’ve had to in the
first place.

00:11:44.630 --> 00:11:50.450
Joyce wrote in her book “Justice Denied”
that she, “... had faith in the system.

00:11:50.450 --> 00:11:53.820
If you did a crime, you did the time.

00:11:53.820 --> 00:11:56.500
If you were innocent, you went free.”

00:11:56.500 --> 00:12:03.850
The thing is -- lives like Joyce Ann Brown’s
have been and will continue to be destroyed

00:12:03.850 --> 00:12:07.590
because we keep getting the same things wrong.

00:12:07.590 --> 00:12:15.630
We fall prey to the same cognitive biases,
analytical fallacies, and just plain thinking

00:12:15.630 --> 00:12:20.590
errors that I’ve been exploring on this
channel for years.

00:12:20.590 --> 00:12:25.560
One day it’s the Einstellung effect -- the
next day it’s confirmation bias, or any

00:12:25.560 --> 00:12:29.540
of dozens of misinterpretations in math.

00:12:29.540 --> 00:12:32.010
I know all of these things.

00:12:32.010 --> 00:12:34.510
YOU know all of these things.

00:12:34.510 --> 00:12:42.820
But not everyone else does -- and their compounding
errors can determine the freedom of people

00:12:42.820 --> 00:12:49.850
named… well, whatever your first, middle,
and last name is.

00:12:49.850 --> 00:12:52.300
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:12:52.300 --> 00:12:56.980
Well the first thing I'm gonna do is spend
some time with my daughter.

00:12:56.980 --> 00:13:00.610
And that very night I'm gonna sleep in the
bed with my momma.

00:13:00.610 --> 00:13:03.020
I've been waitin' on that day a long time.

